WEBVTT

00:00:00.565 --> 00:00:21.341
<v Riley>Hey there. On today's episode, iFixit tears down the new Apple Watch and finds no big repairability news, which leaves us asking: what would you measure before buying gear that has to work at minus twenty-five? Plus, Search and Rescue finds a missing person near Cottonwood Creek, and the Dutch tax authority eyes a Microsoft breakup. Stay with us.

00:00:34.816 --> 00:00:55.376
<v Riley>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities. I'm Riley. It's Wednesday, October 7th, 2026. Reaching you from the heart of the Cariboo, the homeland of the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh nations. Today's theme is Repair Culture &amp; Practical Tech.

00:00:56.376 --> 00:01:17.712
<v Casey>And I'm Casey. Today is about the machines and gear that earn their keep, and what it takes to keep them running. Out at Horsefly Lake it's overcast and about 7 this morning, with a high of 13 and a low of 6, and only a 4 percent chance of rain. 100 Mile House, Williams Lake and Quesnel are all around 6 and overcast. Redstone is the cold spot at about 2.

00:01:18.070 --> 00:01:20.366
<v Riley>Good day to be in a garage. Let's get into the news.

00:01:21.466 --> 00:01:27.466
<v Riley>A lot of today's news is close to home: roads, backcountry, and a municipal election that's already underway.

00:01:29.149 --> 00:02:04.227
<v Riley>A two-vehicle collision on Highway 97 in 100 Mile House sent two people to hospital. The 100 Mile House Free Press reports the RCMP responded on Tuesday, September 29th. The story ran in the paper today, more than a week after the crash. The report as we have it doesn't say what caused the collision, and it doesn't give the condition of the two people. For a community of about 1,900 residents, Highway 97 is both the main street and the main freight route, so any collision there tends to touch more than the people involved.

00:02:05.708 --> 00:02:28.548
<v Casey>Search and Rescue teams found a person who went missing over the weekend in the Cottonwood and Victoria Creek area. My Cariboo Now says multiple teams helped with the search. The person was located. The report doesn't say what condition they were in or how long the search ran. Searches like this lean on volunteers who bring their own trucks, radios and layers, and cover ground where cell signal is patchy at best.

00:02:28.998 --> 00:02:32.398
<v Riley>Multiple teams on one search. That's a lot of volunteer hours.

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:59.352
<v Casey>The Tribune has a Vancouver man who finished a coast-to-coast-and-back tour of Canada on a coffee bike. He pedalled home through a province that had just declared a state of emergency over wildfires. He told the paper the air smelled burnt. He said he couldn't imagine how bad people felt at the actual fire sites. The detail that matters for us is that smoke is now part of the travel forecast, for visitors and for anyone running equipment outdoors in it.

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:18.988
<v Riley>There's also a letter to the Tribune from René Liebaert of Parksville, thanking everyone keeping racing alive in Williams Lake. He's proud of his granddaughter, Presley Liebaert, who races. It's a thank-you to the volunteers, families and track crews who keep a local circuit running, which takes plenty of unpaid weekends.

00:03:19.438 --> 00:03:21.678
<v Casey>Somebody's always turning wrenches in the pits.

00:03:23.258 --> 00:03:41.674
<v Riley>The Esk'etemc First Nation is heading to the polls to elect a chief and three councillors. My Cariboo Now reports the vote is set for Thursday, November 8th. The outlet describes the chief's seat as a possible new chief. The report doesn't say who is running, so that's all we can say about the race.

00:03:43.219 --> 00:04:01.891
<v Casey>Municipal voters have early voting opportunities this week across the Cariboo. My Cariboo Now has the details on where and when. The short version is that early voting exists so people who work shifts, run ranches or live far out don't have to bet everything on a single Saturday. Check your own community's listing before you head out.

00:04:03.325 --> 00:04:24.581
<v Riley>Provincial politics, too. MP Todd Doherty has endorsed Lorne Doerkson for Premier, according to the Tribune. Doerkson is the Cariboo-Chilcotin MLA, and an endorsement from a sitting MP is a signal about where regional Conservative support is gathering. The source doesn't tell us how much it moves the race, so we'll follow it as more comes in.

00:04:26.113 --> 00:04:54.847
<v Casey>Now to the supply chain behind a lot of our gear. Westwin Elements plans to invest 502 million dollars over five years to build a commercial-scale, high-purity nickel refinery in Mississippi. The Northern Miner says it's part of the U.S. push to expand domestic processing of critical minerals. Nickel goes into batteries and steel, and refining is the step where a lot of the leverage sits. Canada digs plenty of the ore, and the question for resource regions like ours is who captures the processing.

00:04:56.435 --> 00:05:29.201
<v Riley>HubSpot is cutting 7 percent of its workforce, roughly 660 people. The CEO says the cuts aren't driven by AI efficiencies, but the company is reorganizing around what it calls AI-driven customer outcomes. Fast Company says investors have spent months worrying that AI will shrink demand for software like HubSpot's, a fear dubbed the "SaaSpocalypse." The company was also removed from the FTSE All-World Index last month. For small businesses here that run their customer lists on these platforms, the vendor's turmoil is their risk too.

00:05:29.651 --> 00:05:33.587
<v Casey>The cut is explained as not about AI, and the reorganization is about AI.

00:05:35.010 --> 00:06:00.626
<v Riley>GitHub says it's rebuilding its Git infrastructure while the service keeps running. The goal is to handle what it calls agent-scale development, meaning far more automated code activity hitting the same systems. That's open-source tooling most of the world's software depends on, being rebuilt in flight. Anyone in the Cariboo who runs a self-hosted setup or a homelab with code in it is downstream of choices like this.

00:06:02.166 --> 00:06:24.430
<v Casey>The Dutch tax authority plans to move away from Microsoft 365 toward on-premises and open source tools. Neowin reports it won't cut Microsoft off entirely and will still rely on tools like Outlook. The stated aim is to reduce reliance on one vendor, not to eliminate it. It's a national version of a question any small office can ask: if the vendor changes terms, what's still yours?

00:06:24.330 --> 00:06:25.290
<v Riley>Partial divorce.

00:06:25.546 --> 00:06:26.882
<v Casey>Those are the worst kind.

00:06:28.426 --> 00:06:57.202
<v Riley>In Lisbon, a practice called Saraiva + Associados is using AI to make design trade-offs visible while there's still time to change them. Architizer notes Portuguese housing costs are up nearly 20 percent. The idea is that every compromise on a residential design has a price, and showing it early lets clients decide before concrete is poured. Housing costs are a live issue in Cariboo towns too, and the same approach would apply to anyone planning a build.

00:06:58.737 --> 00:07:25.273
<v Casey>Fast Company reports on a hiring problem: employers can't tell if they're talking to a human. A report from Western Governors University says 60 percent of U.S. hiring managers say AI is making it harder to evaluate a candidate's real skills. More than 1.2 million people a week visit a Reddit forum called r/recruitinghell. For small employers in towns like Quesnel, where hiring often runs on word of mouth, the personal reference suddenly looks like a feature.

00:07:26.860 --> 00:07:41.788
<v Riley>A practical one. Windows 11's Snipping Tool has a built-in feature for redacting sensitive info from screenshots, Neowin reports. If you send screenshots to a support line or a bank, it hides account numbers before the image leaves your machine.

00:07:43.392 --> 00:07:54.152
<v Casey>And earlier this week, New Atlas covered a soft, wind-up elastic robot that jumps continuously when fed infrared light. No battery, no motor in the usual sense. It's a lab demonstration for now.

00:07:54.602 --> 00:07:59.658
<v Riley>Which brings us to the gear that actually has to work. Right after a community spotlight.

00:08:00.758 --> 00:08:14.798
<v Casey>The Boys and Girls Club of Williams Lake has run after-school programs, summer camps and youth mentorship for local families since 1994. Programs are low-to-no cost and open to all kids and teens. You can find them at bgcwilliamslake.com.

00:08:15.144 --> 00:08:17.872
<v Riley>Open to every kid, no gatekeeping. Good model.

00:08:18.052 --> 00:08:18.964
<v Casey>Now, about that gear.

00:08:20.064 --> 00:08:33.664
<v Riley>iFixit tore down the new Apple Watch Series 12 and Ultra 4. Their verdict: no big repairability news. The headline on the teardown says it reveals a gap in right-to-repair coverage. So what was the star rating measuring?

00:08:33.943 --> 00:08:37.495
<v Casey>Probably the screen. Spec sheets measure what's easy to photograph.

00:08:38.011 --> 00:08:54.131
<v Riley>Take a ranch. A truck, an ATV, a generator, a handheld GPS, maybe a homelab box running cameras. Nobody buys any of it on spec sheet numbers alone, but that's what the listing gives you. Horsepower. Battery life in hours. A five-star average from people who've owned it for a month.

00:08:54.708 --> 00:09:00.236
<v Casey>A month. The failure happens in year three, at minus twenty-five, forty kilometres up a service road.

00:09:00.827 --> 00:09:14.827
<v Riley>So measure that. Time to repair. Can a person with a basic toolkit get it running again? Is the manual public? Are parts sold separately? iFixit already scores repairability. It's a number built to measure the thing the spec sheet skips.

00:09:15.382 --> 00:09:28.638
<v Casey>Which is also a proxy. A repair score tells you the design is open. It doesn't tell you the part is in stock in Williams Lake, or that anyone local has done the job before. Give a proxy a few years and people start buying to the score.

00:09:29.273 --> 00:09:35.033
<v Riley>Then add a second measure. Ask who's fixed one nearby. That's data too. It's just not on a website.

00:09:35.691 --> 00:09:46.595
<v Casey>Right. The number that matters is "who do I call." For a truck that's a mechanic you know. For a homelab build it's whoever on the forum answered the last question, and whether they're still around.

00:09:47.270 --> 00:10:09.526
<v Riley>We've been in this territory before. On July 22nd it was who has the legal right to modify and repair. In August it was hospital equipment locked behind service keys, and last month it was whether easier repair tools teach the skills or replace them. Today's different. Say you've already won the legal fight. What would you measure to decide whether to buy?

00:10:09.820 --> 00:10:19.820
<v Casey>Downtime. Not uptime percentages, downtime. How many days was the thing dead, and what did the dead days cost? On a ranch a down tractor in haying season costs more than the repair bill.

00:10:20.448 --> 00:10:27.840
<v Riley>Good. Track it per machine. Hours of use, hours down, dollars and days to fix. A notebook in the shop works, or a spreadsheet.

00:10:28.171 --> 00:10:30.339
<v Casey>A spreadsheet nobody updates after March.

00:10:30.859 --> 00:10:41.299
<v Riley>Then make it one line on the receipt folder. Date, what broke, days down. Three fields. Commercial fleets track exactly this kind of thing, and the logging habit is the real tool.

00:10:41.931 --> 00:10:48.627
<v Casey>Fleets also have a person whose job it is. Plenty of rural programs run on a logbook that died when the enthusiast moved away.

00:10:49.168 --> 00:10:54.728
<v Riley>Then it lives on the machine. A sticker in the cab. Write it where the next owner will see it.

00:10:55.067 --> 00:10:56.203
<v Casey>Huh. That one might survive.

00:10:56.887 --> 00:11:11.183
<v Riley>Signal matters here too. Spotty coverage rules out gear that needs the cloud to function. A trail camera that only works through an app. A tracker that stops reporting when a server goes dark. Measure the thing with the cable unplugged.

00:11:11.740 --> 00:11:29.812
<v Casey>The cable-unplugged test. What happens when the vendor's server is gone? The Dutch tax authority just made a version of that bet at national scale, moving toward on-premises and open source because they didn't like the dependence. They're keeping Outlook, so even they didn't go all the way. A ranch can do the small version.

00:11:30.430 --> 00:11:43.422
<v Riley>And a homelab build is the small version done properly. A box in the shed running your own camera storage, local weather logging, whatever. The measure is whether it keeps working when the internet drops for a day.

00:11:44.013 --> 00:11:49.005
<v Casey>And whether you can rebuild it. Not "does it run," but how long to restore if the drive dies.

00:11:49.293 --> 00:11:52.813
<v Riley>Restore time. Fine. Test it once. Pull the drive, time the rebuild.

00:11:52.993 --> 00:11:54.137
<v Casey>Nobody tests it once.

00:11:54.819 --> 00:12:02.227
<v Riley>Then that's your honest number: how many people who own a backup have ever restored from it. I won't pretend that's high.

00:12:02.516 --> 00:12:04.772
<v Casey>Low, I'd guess. I don't have a figure for it.

00:12:05.336 --> 00:12:22.384
<v Riley>Neither do I. Back to the watch. The teardown makes a smaller point. A product can look fine on the headline and still hide the part that matters. iFixit says there's no big repairability news, and for a product that's been through this many generations, that is the news. Nothing improved.

00:12:22.969 --> 00:12:36.873
<v Casey>And a watch is a luxury. The same logic hits the equipment people depend on. Newer vehicles lean on software-locked parts. The older fleet around here is fixable at a kitchen table. Reading a spec sheet on a new truck, you won't see which category it's in.

00:12:37.412 --> 00:12:44.204
<v Riley>Ask the dealer. Can an independent shop run diagnostics on this model? Get the answer in writing before you sign.

00:12:44.719 --> 00:12:59.503
<v Casey>And ask the independent shop too. In a town the size of Williams Lake, about 10,700 people, there aren't many of them, and their answer matters more than the dealer's. In 100 Mile House, at about 1,900, you may be asking one person.

00:13:00.175 --> 00:13:06.071
<v Riley>One person who's seen every failure mode on that road. That's a better data set than any star rating.

00:13:06.368 --> 00:13:10.056
<v Casey>Until they retire. Then the data set is gone and nobody wrote it down.

00:13:10.586 --> 00:13:21.738
<v Riley>Which is the argument for writing it down. The failures that matter here are local. Cold starts. Washboard roads. Dust. Fine silt in a charging port. A national review site won't test for any of that.

00:13:22.381 --> 00:13:29.501
<v Casey>Nobody reviews a generator by leaving it in a barn at minus thirty for a week. We do that to it by accident, and then we find out.

00:13:30.074 --> 00:13:37.778
<v Riley>So the Cariboo rating is the one we build ourselves. Hours down. Days to fix. Who fixed it. Whether it needed a network. Four fields.

00:13:38.094 --> 00:13:39.270
<v Casey>You said three a minute ago.

00:13:39.596 --> 00:13:41.972
<v Riley>The network one is yes or no. It doesn't count.

00:13:42.152 --> 00:13:43.112
<v Casey>Ha. It counts the most.

00:13:43.292 --> 00:13:44.700
<v Riley>Fine. It counts the most.

00:13:45.381 --> 00:14:02.637
<v Casey>Here's my problem with all of it. Every one of these measures can drift too. Say a local sheet starts ranking brands by days down. Dealers learn to get the part to you fast by courier, so the number looks good, and the machine is still failing every season. Now the proxy is days to fix, not whether it breaks.

00:14:03.191 --> 00:14:11.431
<v Riley>Then log the count of failures too, not only the duration. Two numbers pull against each other. Fast fixes of the same part, three times, shows up.

00:14:11.727 --> 00:14:13.623
<v Casey>It shows up if someone reads the log.

00:14:14.278 --> 00:14:19.790
<v Riley>Someone will. A rancher looking at a second truck will read it. The incentive is their own money.

00:14:20.439 --> 00:14:28.799
<v Casey>Their own money, and only if the log exists in the first place. The incentive to start it is weak and the incentive to read it is strong. That's a gap.

00:14:29.147 --> 00:14:32.635
<v Riley>Which a shared sheet closes. One person starts it, forty people read it.

00:14:33.152 --> 00:14:38.064
<v Casey>Forty people read it and one person maintains it. That's the same gap with more readers.

00:14:38.620 --> 00:14:57.300
<v Riley>Then the maintainer should be a group that already keeps records. Search and rescue teams in the Cottonwood area run on equipment that has to work in the cold, and every search ends with a debrief. They'd know which radios, trucks and trackers fail in the field, and they'd know it from use, not from reviews.

00:14:57.908 --> 00:15:07.876
<v Casey>I'd start there. Ask a local volunteer group what they'd put in that sheet. Their answer might not match anything we've said today. Then see if anyone's willing to keep it going.

00:15:08.464 --> 00:15:16.928
<v Riley>Same for the racing crowd in Williams Lake. People who keep engines running on a budget every weekend have a better failure database than any magazine.

00:15:17.288 --> 00:15:21.144
<v Casey>They also have strong opinions about brands. That's a bias to log too.

00:15:21.439 --> 00:15:25.567
<v Riley>I'd take the bias. At least it comes from a person who bought the thing.

00:15:26.140 --> 00:15:33.252
<v Casey>The disagreement, then. You think better personal numbers get most of the way there. Downtime, restore time, who's fixed one nearby.

00:15:33.581 --> 00:15:37.637
<v Riley>Yes. People share what they log. That's how forum knowledge started.

00:15:38.172 --> 00:15:49.364
<v Casey>I think those numbers help the person already paying attention. Most people buy on the headline because the headline is all they're shown. Better personal metrics don't fix a market that sells stars.

00:15:49.680 --> 00:15:53.000
<v Riley>The market isn't the only source. Neighbours are a source.

00:15:53.283 --> 00:15:55.987
<v Casey>Sometimes. I'd want to see it before I counted on it.

00:15:56.517 --> 00:16:09.821
<v Riley>Fair enough to leave it there. One small experiment for next month: a shared sheet of what broke and how long it took, for local ranchers, volunteers and anyone running gear in the backcountry. Four columns. See who adds a row.

00:16:10.334 --> 00:16:20.038
<v Casey>And see who adds a second row. The first row is easy. If you've got a repair log idea, a gear failure story, or a correction, write to us at feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a.

00:16:20.342 --> 00:16:21.534
<v Riley>Have a great rest of your day.

00:16:21.866 --> 00:16:23.234
<v Casey>Check the oil before you go.